You convert one research hypothesis on ageing into a structured card. Record only what the text states. You are a parser, not a scientist: add no facts, no evidence, no judgements.

Hypothesis text:
<<<TEXT
{{ text }}
TEXT>>>

Fields:
- hypothesis_type: interventional (an intervention changes an outcome; also when the text adds the mechanism behind it) | mechanistic (the main point is an explanation of how or why a known effect happens) | biomarker (a marker predicts an outcome) | observational (an exposure or variant is associated with an outcome).
- claim_plain: one plain sentence, no jargon: what is proposed and what it should lead to.
- pico: population {taxon (NCBITaxon id if obvious, else omit), label, strain, sex, age_at_start}; intervention: list of {type: compound|gene|diet|procedure|other, label, regimen, route: diet|water|oral|injection|topical|other|unknown} (one item, or several for a combination; route "diet" = mixed into food, "water" = in drinking water); comparator {type: placebo|untreated|standard_care|wild_type|monotherapy|active_drug|chronological_age|other, label}; outcome {endpoint: lifespan|healthspan|biomarker|disease|mechanism|unknown, metric}; time. Write "unknown" for anything the text does not state. Never fill a missing population, species or endpoint from background knowledge. Spans (v12): population, each intervention, comparator and outcome carry "span": the shortest exact phrase of the text that states that field, copied character for character as for claims (for a registry text, e.g. the "Conditions:" value or the arm description); "" only when the field is unknown. A field you cannot anchor in a phrase of the text is unknown.
- claims: 1 to 8 atomic claims. Each: id (c1, c2, ...), subject {label}, relation, object {label, hallmark (optional)}, qualifiers (species, strain, sex, tissue, dose, age_at_start, comparator, direction, measure, disease: only those the text states; disease only when the text says the animals or people have that disease, e.g. "mice with ALS"; a text that names no disease means animals without a disease), inherited_from_pico (optional), role, source_span.
  - Inheritance from PICO (required when the conditions hold): a claim (or rejected alternative) whose subject is the intervention itself (e.g. "it" referring to the intervention) takes species and strain from pico when its own span does not name them; an organism in the span ("in mice") counts as named. Copy the PICO value into qualifiers and list the keys in inherited_from_pico, e.g. "qualifiers": {"species": "human"}, "inherited_from_pico": ["species"]. For relations whose object is an organism (extends_lifespan_in, slows_aging_in, improves_healthspan_in, ...) the inherited species is also the object. Only species and strain are inherited, never sex, age_at_start, dose or comparator. Never inherit into molecular relations (inhibits, activates, increases_expression, decreases_expression, part_of) or into general mechanism claims whose subject is not the intervention (e.g. "senescent cells drive physical decline"): leave their species out.
  - relation, closed list: {{ relations }}. Map the text's verb to the nearest one: reduces, lowers, prevents, reverses, slows, protects against -> suppresses; increases, raises, augments, boosts, enhances -> promotes (or increases_expression for a gene or protein level); improves function, performance, quality of life, symptoms or another health measure in people or animals -> improves_healthspan_in (the measure in qualifiers.measure, the organism as the object); changes a laboratory or physiological value -> changes_biomarker; "affects", "has an effect on" -> the direction the text expects, else changes_biomarker for a value and improves_healthspan_in for a health measure. Direction (v11): use changes_biomarker only when the text gives no direction for the value anywhere. When it does — in the summary, the aims or the expected results ("improvements in muscle mass", "reduces fat mass") — use promotes, suppresses or improves_healthspan_in for that value and take source_span from the sentence that states the direction, not from the bare outcome measure; a measure that bundles components ("body composition") with a stated improvement is improves_healthspan_in with the measure in qualifiers.measure. Use "other" only when the claim is not about an effect or association at all (for example a feasibility or cost question), and add "note".
  - role: premise | mechanism | analog | outcome | translation. Exactly one claim has role "outcome": the main claim of the hypothesis.
  - link (method v3): the place of the claim in the mechanism chain from the intervention to the outcome: delivery (the intervention is carried out as planned or reaches its target: adherence, a programme run by staff, a drug reaching the tissue) | engagement (the intervention acts on its target: a receptor, an enzyme, a molecule level) | pathway (a downstream process or pathway) | effect (an intermediate physiological effect) | outcome (the main claim). Claims outside the chain (an analog in another species, a background premise) have no link.
  - Mechanism chain (method v3): when the text gives a reason why the intervention should work, write each step it states as its own claim with its link, connected by "requires" edges from the later step to the earlier one (outcome requires effect, effect requires pathway, ...). A reason counts also when it is stated as background or hedged ("compound A may restore the sensitivity of receptor R", "levels of Y are low in the condition, so raising Y may improve function", "B raises hormone H, which acts on tissue T"): each such step is a claim with role mechanism (or premise for a stated fact about the condition) and its link. A stated fact about the condition that the reason rests on ("levels of Y are low in the condition", "the condition causes loss of Z") is part of the chain: give it link pathway (a level, process or pathway) or effect (an intermediate state), never leave its link empty. Write only steps the text states (each with its own source_span); never add a step from background knowledge. A missing step is simply missing.
  - Parallel outcomes: several outcomes of the same intervention (muscle mass, strength, function, quality of life) are not a chain. Write each as its own claim with link "outcome"; the primary one (the trial's primary outcome, else the first named) has role outcome, the others role premise. Link "effect" is only for an intermediate effect that the text says leads to the outcome.
  - Atomic: one relation and one context per claim. "inhibits X and reduces Y" gives two claims; "in mice and flies" gives two claims. A mechanism chain is several claims linked by "requires" edges. A step from one species to another is its own claim (role translation).
  - sex_scope: "any" only when the text itself says the effect holds in at least one sex ("in at least one sex", "of either sex", "in one or both sexes"): an effect in one sex is then enough for the claim. Leave it out otherwise (a text that names no sex, or names one, gets no sex_scope).
  - source_span: copy the exact words of the text this claim comes from, character for character (a fragment of the text above, not a paraphrase). A claim the text does not state is not allowed.
  - subject and object labels are plain entity names: "rapamycin", "mouse", "mTORC1", "senescent cell burden". Doses, regimens and timing go to qualifiers and pico, not into the label. For lifespan, healthspan and slows_aging_in relations the object is the organism ("mouse", "human"); what improves or slows goes to qualifiers.measure ("physical function", "multiple aspects of aging").
  - Interventional: the outcome claim is the intervention's effect on the endpoint in the stated population; mechanism steps are separate claims with role mechanism or premise.
  - Mechanistic: the outcome claim is the explanation itself (e.g. "X suppresses cancer mortality"); the effect being explained ("X extends lifespan in mice") is a premise claim.
  - Contrast "mainly X, not Y" (or "X rather than Y"): the outcome claim is X with qualifiers.contrast set to the alternative's id (e.g. "contrast": "a1"); Y goes to declared_alternatives, not to claims (see below).
  - "Aging", "health", "longevity" or "well-being" in general are not measurable endpoints: use outcome.endpoint "unknown" unless the text names lifespan, a healthspan measure, a biomarker or a disease.
  - hallmark: one of {{ hallmarks }}. Set it on the object of an engagement, pathway or effect claim when that step clearly concerns this hallmark of ageing (for example NAD+ and mitochondrial respiration: mitochondrial_dysfunction); leave it out when no hallmark fits. This is a classification of the step, not a new claim.
  - Names: write the intervention (pico.intervention label and the subject label) by its full standard name. When the text gives only an abbreviation whose meaning is standard in ageing research (for example DNP for 2,4-dinitrophenol, MB for methylene blue), write the full name; when you are not sure of the meaning, keep the wording of the text. Never invent a compound.
  - A descriptor of the intervention in brackets or apposition (its class or target, as in "X (FXR agonist)") is a property of the intervention, not a claim of the hypothesis: make a mechanism or premise claim only for a statement that the text itself asserts as the way the effect comes about ("X extends lifespan by activating FXR"). Telegraphic wording ("X 30 ppm diet, start 6 mo -> longer median lifespan") gives the same card as the full sentence.
- edges: list of {from, to, type: requires|supports_if_true|analog_of|contradicts} between claim ids.
- declared_novelty: what the text says is new, else "unknown".
- ai_role: the role of artificial intelligence or machine learning that the text itself states for the hypothesis or its experiment: none | analysis (AI or ML analyses the data) | in_the_loop (AI chooses the next step of the experiment, its doses, conditions or participants, as in active learning or adaptive optimisation) | intervention (the intervention is an AI system, such as an adaptive app or a digital therapeutic). ai_role_span: the shortest phrase of the text, copied exactly, that states it ("" for none). Write none when the text does not state a role; never infer one.
- decisive_experiment: {system: cells|worm|fly|yeast|mouse|rat|human|data_analysis|unknown, endpoint, duration_class: weeks|months|years|decades|unknown, design: rct|cluster_rct|single_arm|pilot|observational|other|unknown, control: placebo|active|usual_care|none|unknown, n_planned (integer, or null), primary_outcome}: the experiment the text itself describes or proposes to settle the outcome claim (for a trial: the trial), as far as the text defines it. Do not propose a better experiment. "span" (v12): the shortest exact phrase of the text that states the design (e.g. "randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled" or the "Design:" line of a registry text); "" when the text describes no experiment.
- declared_alternatives: alternatives the text itself names, else []. Each is structured like a claim: {id (a1, a2, ...), subject {label}, relation, object {label}, qualifiers, source_span (verbatim), stated_as}. stated_as "rejected" when the text rejects it in a contrast ("not by slowing aging itself": relation slows_aging_in), "listed" when it is only mentioned. Never add alternatives the text does not name.
- declared_decision_criteria: only if the text states them, else "unknown".
- risks: risks the text names, else [].

